---
import floors from '../../data/probe/floor.json'
import probe from '../../data/probe/results2.json'
import { floorColour, floorScale } from '../scripts/orbit.js'

// Beat 4 states published floor figures, so like beat 1 it draws them from floor.json,
// the file floor.py wrote for exactly this. dataset.json keeps the unrounded quotient and
// differs from the probe at the second decimal on 19 of the 57 rows, so every figure here
// is printed to one place, which is a place the two files agree on for each subject named.
// The copy test holds that agreement and names the two rows where it does not hold.
const rows = [...floors].sort((a, b) => a.floor - b.floor)

// floor.json stores the floor as a change against the clean baseline. It is published
// here as a multiple of that baseline instead: the rule sits at 1, the sign change is a
// crossing of the rule, and a plain log axis carries the whole range with no linear patch
// around zero. The plan asked for a symmetric log scale, which is what a delta spanning
// zero needs. A multiple never reaches zero and does not need one.
const mult = (r) => 1 + r.floor
const fmt = (r) => mult(r).toFixed(1)

const LO = Math.min(...rows.map(mult)) / 1.15
const HI = Math.max(...rows.map(mult)) * 1.15
const SPAN = Math.log10(HI) - Math.log10(LO)
const X = (v) => ((Math.log10(v) - Math.log10(LO)) / SPAN) * 100
const RULE = X(1)
const TICKS = [0.5, 1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 50]

// A subject sitting almost on the rule still gets a visible mark, drawn on its own side.
const width = (r) => Math.max(0.15, Math.abs(X(mult(r)) - RULE))
const left = (r) => (r.floor < 0 ? RULE - width(r) : RULE)

// The same two ramps the ring at the top of the page is drawn with, so this is that mark
// unrolled onto one axis rather than a second reading of the same rows. Warm below the
// rule, cool above it, and each ramp stretched to the extreme its own side reaches.
const ramp = floorScale(rows)
const tone = (r) => floorColour(r.floor, r.floor < 0 ? 0.92 : 0.66, ramp)

const below = rows.filter((r) => r.floor < 0).length
const above = rows.length - below

const lowest = rows[0]
const highest = rows.at(-1)
const lead = rows.find((r) => r.article === 'Elizabeth II')
const maxDeath = rows
  .filter((r) => r.class === 'death')
  .reduce((a, b) => (b.floor > a.floor ? b : a))

// Labelled directly on the mark rather than through a legend: the two ends of the
// distribution, the highest of the deaths, and the subject this page opened on. Three of
// the four are picked by the rows, so the label and the mark under it cannot drift apart.
const LABELLED = [lowest.article, lead.article, maxDeath.article, highest.article]

const fast = probe.reduce((a, b) => (b.t50 < a.t50 ? b : a))
const slow = probe.reduce((a, b) => (b.t50 > a.t50 ? b : a))

// Pearson r between log peak views and the floor, recomputed from the rows rather than
// carried across from the spec, so the printed figure is the file's.
const xs = rows.map((r) => Math.log10(r.peak))
const ys = rows.map((r) => r.floor)
const mx = xs.reduce((a, b) => a + b) / xs.length
const my = ys.reduce((a, b) => a + b) / ys.length
let cov = 0
let vx = 0
let vy = 0
for (let i = 0; i < xs.length; i++) {
  cov += (xs[i] - mx) * (ys[i] - my)
  vx += (xs[i] - mx) ** 2
  vy += (ys[i] - my) ** 2
}
const rText = Math.abs(cov / Math.sqrt(vx * vy)).toFixed(2)

// Spec 11 left the class table open: politics at three subjects and sport at five are too
// thin to state, and either the event list grows or those classes come out of it. Growing
// the list is a live network run, so the beat states a class figure only where the class
// carries THICK subjects. That leaves deaths and scandals. The rest are drawn and unnamed,
// and the page says so rather than letting a reader take the set for two classes.
const THICK = 15
const classes = [...new Set(rows.map((r) => r.class))]
const countOf = (c) => rows.filter((r) => r.class === c).length
const thin = classes.filter((c) => countOf(c) < THICK)
const thinnest = Math.max(...thin.map(countOf))

const overDeaths = rows.filter((r) => r.floor > maxDeath.floor)
const scandalsOver = overDeaths.filter((r) => r.class === 'scandal').length
---

<section class="beat">
  <p class="body">
    The fall varies, and it varies in one direction. The quickest in the set is half gone in {fast.t50} days and the slowest in {slow.t50} days, and every one of them falls.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
    A year on they do not agree on direction. {rows.length} subjects have a clean baseline to be measured against. {below} of them end the year below it and {above} above.
  </p>

  <figure class="fig">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div
        class="plot"
        role="img"
        aria-label="Every subject that carries a clean baseline, a year after its event, as a multiple of that baseline, sorted low to high on a logarithmic scale"
      >
        <div class="axis">
          {TICKS.map((t) => <span class="tick" style={`left:${X(t)}%`}>{t}</span>)}
        </div>
        <div class="rows">
          {TICKS.map((t) => <span class="grid" style={`left:${X(t)}%`}></span>)}
          <span class="rule" style={`left:${RULE}%`}></span>
          {rows.map((r) => (
            <div
              class:list={['row', { named: LABELLED.includes(r.article) }]}
              data-mark
              data-side={r.floor < 0 ? 'below' : 'above'}
            >
              <span
                class="bar"
                style={`left:${left(r)}%;width:${width(r)}%;background:${tone(r)}`}
              ></span>
              {LABELLED.includes(r.article) && (
                <span
                  class="tag"
                  style={`left:${RULE}%;transform:translateX(${r.floor < 0 ? '-100%' : '0'})`}
                >{r.article} {fmt(r)} times</span>
              )}
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <span class="rule-label" style={`left:${RULE}%`}>clean baseline</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      The ring at the top of this page, unrolled onto one axis and kept in the same order.
      Every subject a year after its event, as a multiple of its own clean baseline. The
      rule is that baseline. The scale is logarithmic. Sorted, so the colour change is the
      sign change.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p class="body">
    A year on, {highest.article}'s article was running at {fmt(highest)} times its clean baseline. {lowest.article}'s was running at {fmt(lowest)} times. {lead.article}, the subject this page opened on, sits at {fmt(lead)} times.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
    Spike size does not sort this either. Across the {rows.length} subjects, r = −{rText} between log peak views and the floor.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
    {countOf('death')} of the {rows.length} subjects are deaths and {countOf('scandal')} are scandals. The highest floor among the deaths is {maxDeath.article} at {fmt(maxDeath)} times. {overDeaths.length} subjects sit higher, and {scandalsOver} of them are scandals.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
    The set holds {classes.length} classes. The other {thin.length} carry {thinnest} subjects or fewer. That is too thin to state, so they are drawn here and not named.
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .beat {
    padding-bottom: 6rem;
  }

  .body {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    font-size: 1.06rem;
  }

  .fig {
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0;
  }

  figcaption {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 2.75rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .wrap {
    padding: 0 1.75rem;
  }

  .plot {
    position: relative;
    padding-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .axis {
    position: relative;
    height: 1.3rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .tick {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .rows {
    position: relative;
    padding-top: 0.7rem;
  }

  .grid {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    width: 1px;
    background: var(--rule);
  }

  .rule {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    width: 1px;
    background: var(--ink);
  }

  .row {
    position: relative;
    height: 7px;
    margin-bottom: 5px;
  }

  .row.named {
    margin-top: 1.6rem;
  }

  .bar {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    height: 100%;
  }

  /* Anchored at the rule and turned outward from it. Every bar has one end on the rule, so
     the tag sits over its own bar's own edge and never runs across the rule or off the
     frame, whichever side of it the subject is on. */
  .tag {
    position: absolute;
    bottom: 100%;
    padding: 0 0.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .rule-label {
    position: absolute;
    top: 100%;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  @media (max-width: 34rem) {
    .wrap {
      padding: 0;
    }

    .tag,
    .tick,
    .rule-label {
      font-size: 0.72rem;
    }
  }
</style>
